ly living up to what  the ICJ the UN's highest court is now  impose  upon it Antonia movi is executive  director of legal action worldwide a  non-profit group focused on human rights  law she joins us now live from Beirut  what's your reaction to this ruling  today is momentous day I mean who would  have thought that the International  Court of Justice has made a decision a  unanimous decision a panel of seventeen  judges one appointed by selected by  Myanmar and one by Gambia of provisional  measures to prevent acts of genocide and  that Myanmar military must not commit  further acts of genocide and as we heard  earlier from your reporter not to  destroy evidence and to report back to  the court I mean it was incredible and  we represent rahega refugees who are  residing in Bangladesh and we saw their  faces and their reactions that the world  has taken note and the highest court in  the land has has made this decision it's  really very very landmark today and  going forward what does that mean you  know the rahaga have been seeking  justice we've been helping them to try  to obtain that and this is an incredibly  important step even though it may take  years for the full trial to to go ahead  yeah but the as I understand it that the  court's decision is unenforceable I mean  me and not does not have to comply it  could just ignore the ruling well it is  a legally binding decision and I think  let's go back to the hearings in in  December and some suci herself went to  represent her country and I think that's  very significant that shows us that she  and Myanmar have actually engaged in  these proceedings so it is legally  binding of course it's a million dollar  question will Myanmar comply they do  have very strict reporting provisions  that have been requested by the court  what happens if they don't it would have  to go to the UN Security Council I think  here is going to be really important  that we continue international pressure  on Myanmar to abide by its legal  obligations and also those that are in  Bangladesh watch to see what happens to  the ringer and when they come if they  have to  still Myanmar into Bangladesh there we  take their stories and hear what they  have to say so I think now we're going  to see what will be the reaction and of  course to till now we haven't seen a  response from an sung Suchi to the  court's decision yeah I was going to ask  you what this means for Aung San su Qi  given that she denied that a genocide  had occurred in Myanmar you know I would  love to be a fly on the wall right now  and seeing what designs sung su Qi think  to this you know Human Rights icon and  there she was at The Hague now defending  the actions of the Myanmar military and  I was there in The Hague with some of  the ringer refugees who had fled during  the 2017 clearance operations  some of them who'd lost a 20-year old  widow it had nine members of her family  killed and subjected to very serious  sexual violence and who'd wanted to just  reach out in the court against an sung  su Chi I think that she had said before  to the world she didn't know what was  happening  she cannot deny that and the highest  court in the land has also said to take  action now to prevent further acts to  protect 600,000 reinga who still remain  in recurrence stay tuned Myanmar and I  think that's incredibly important we  shouldn't forget that but there's still  many reinga still residing in Myanmar  and that's absolutely crucial that  decision for them today  okay many thanks indeed good to talk to  you Antonia movi their executive  director of legal action worldwide

